1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,053
Lesson 5.3.

2
00:00:02,433 --> 00:00:04,059
Extropic's TSUs.

3
00:00:05,183 --> 00:00:10,118
Extropic calls a thermodynamic
sampling unit, or TSU, a chip that runs

4
00:00:10,118 --> 00:00:14,347
Gibbs sampling in hardware over
a graph of random variables.

5
00:00:14,727 --> 00:00:17,805
Its program is an energy,
and its output is samples.

6
00:00:18,185 --> 00:00:24,136
In this lesson we go through its pieces:
the random primitives, the X0 and XTR-0 test

7
00:00:24,136 --> 00:00:29,177
chips, and the Z1 chip, with more
than two hundred sixty thousand pbits.

8
00:00:30,283 --> 00:00:32,585
The TSU does not only have pbits.

9
00:00:32,965 --> 00:00:35,882
A pbit samples a bit
with sigmoid probability.

10
00:00:36,262 --> 00:00:38,988
A pdit samples a categorical variable with

11
00:00:38,988 --> 00:00:41,779
several values, with
softmax probabilities.

12
00:00:42,159 --> 00:00:44,711
A pmode samples a continuous Gaussian

13
00:00:44,711 --> 00:00:47,813
variable, and a pmog,
a mixture of Gaussians.

14
00:00:48,193 --> 00:00:51,620
With these four pieces one
builds discrete, continuous

15
00:00:51,620 --> 00:00:54,793
and hybrid models, always
with local conditionals.

16
00:00:55,917 --> 00:01:01,049
The first chips, X0 and XTR-0,
served to validate the primitives.

17
00:01:01,429 --> 00:01:04,773
They use CMOS samplers in
the subthreshold regime,

18
00:01:04,773 --> 00:01:07,649
where transistor noise
is large and useful.

19
00:01:08,029 --> 00:01:11,505
Since each device is somewhat
different, each pbit is

20
00:01:11,505 --> 00:01:14,587
calibrated: according to
the syllabus, Bayesian

21
00:01:14,587 --> 00:01:18,719
optimization reaches a probability
error of about five percent.

22
00:01:19,099 --> 00:01:23,039
The complete system is a loop:
a CPU defines the problem,

23
00:01:23,039 --> 00:01:26,564
an FPGA translates and
reads, and the chip samples.

24
00:01:27,683 --> 00:01:31,491
The Z1 chip has two hundred
sixty-nine thousand five hundred

25
00:01:31,491 --> 00:01:35,553
sixty-eight pbits, each with
sixteen couplings to its neighbors.

26
00:01:35,933 --> 00:01:39,915
It runs at more than fifty megahertz
and consumes under one watt.

27
00:01:40,295 --> 00:01:44,006
That gives more than ten
trillion updates per second,

28
00:01:44,006 --> 00:01:47,366
and at most seventy-four
femtojoules per update.

29
00:01:47,746 --> 00:01:50,736
It comes in M.2 and PCIe cards, and in

30
00:01:50,736 --> 00:01:54,041
cluster systems aiming at a billion pbits.

31
00:01:55,150 --> 00:01:58,980
Programming a TSU means writing
an energy over its fixed graph.

32
00:01:59,360 --> 00:02:03,665
The couplings and fields are
loaded into each pbit's registers,

33
00:02:03,665 --> 00:02:07,628
an effective temperature is
set, and chromatic Gibbs runs.

34
00:02:08,008 --> 00:02:12,643
Some variables can be clamped as
evidence, just like in invertible logic.

35
00:02:13,023 --> 00:02:15,483
Every so many sweeps,
samples are read out.

36
00:02:15,863 --> 00:02:19,392
If the model does not fit in
a degree-sixteen graph, it

37
00:02:19,392 --> 00:02:22,536
has to be embedded, as we
will see in lesson 5.7.

38
00:02:23,650 --> 00:02:25,721
Let's run the numbers
with the stated figures.

39
00:02:26,101 --> 00:02:29,951
Two hundred sixty-nine
thousand pbits at fifty

40
00:02:29,951 --> 00:02:34,135
megahertz are about 1.3 ×
10¹³ updates per second.

41
00:02:34,515 --> 00:02:41,033
With one watt, the energy per
update is at most 7.4 × 10⁻¹⁴ J.

42
00:02:41,413 --> 00:02:45,079
Compared with the Landauer
limit, that is about twenty-six

43
00:02:45,079 --> 00:02:48,240
million times more, but
compared with an estimated

44
00:02:48,240 --> 00:02:52,538
seventy-picojoule digital update,
it is about a thousand times less.

45
00:02:53,650 --> 00:02:59,063
In the course we will use THRML
to simulate TSU programs on GPU,

46
00:02:59,063 --> 00:03:04,391
build denoising models sampled
by the TSU in unit nine, and see

47
00:03:04,391 --> 00:03:08,873
the Z1T architecture for
language models in unit ten.

48
00:03:09,253 --> 00:03:12,614
In lab eight you will
build a degree-sixteen

49
00:03:12,614 --> 00:03:16,280
graph, color it and
estimate updates per second.

50
00:03:17,383 --> 00:03:21,635
Careful: the seventy-four-femtojoule
figure is a bound derived

51
00:03:21,635 --> 00:03:25,407
from the stated power and
frequency, not a measurement.

52
00:03:25,787 --> 00:03:28,615
The Z1 graph is fixed, of degree sixteen.

53
00:03:28,995 --> 00:03:32,341
Calibration leaves an error
on the order of five percent.

54
00:03:32,721 --> 00:03:37,326
And the energy per update does not
include input, output or the host.

55
00:03:38,450 --> 00:03:43,513
In summary: a TSU runs chromatic
Gibbs in hardware over a fixed

56
00:03:43,513 --> 00:03:48,013
graph, with four primitives:
pbit, pdit, pmode and pmog.

57
00:03:48,393 --> 00:03:53,778
X0 and XTR-0 validated the subthreshold
samplers and their calibration.

58
00:03:54,158 --> 00:03:58,132
Z1 scales to two hundred
sixty-nine thousand degree-sixteen

59
00:03:58,132 --> 00:04:02,106
pbits, with a bound of seventy-four
femtojoules per update.

60
00:04:03,217 --> 00:04:07,078
To practice: compute
Z1's updates per second.

61
00:04:07,458 --> 00:04:12,501
Choose the primitive for a ten-class
label and for a pixel intensity.

62
00:04:12,881 --> 00:04:15,730
Compute the sweeps per
second with four colors.

63
00:04:16,110 --> 00:04:19,608
And explain why each pbit
needs its own calibration.
